  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The cranberry leafcutter bee (Megachile addenda) is a solitary bee species in the family Megachilidae, native to New England and the northeastern United States. These medium-sized bees are named for their habit of cutting semicircular pieces from leaves to construct their nesting cells.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Leaf-cutting behavior",
      "description": "Distinctive habit of cutting neat, semi-circular pieces from leaves to line and cap their nesting tubes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Body size and color",
      "description": "Medium-sized bee (8-12 mm) with dark brown to black coloration and a robust build",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Abdominal pollen brush",
      "description": "Dense scopae (pollen-collecting hairs) on the underside of the abdomen for carrying pollen",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Cranberry leafcutter bees are found in open woodlands, gardens, and agricultural areas including cranberry bogs throughout New England, where they nest in hollow stems, cavities, or provided bee hotels.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "These bees are valuable pollinators for cranberry crops in New England, and a single bee can cut up to 40 leaf pieces in a day to construct the intricate cells where it lays its eggs.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
